digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study proposes rational CPL emitter design by tailoring the structural dimensionality of lead-free indium-based chiral metal-halide hybrids from 0D to 3D using size- and polarization-tuned chiral organic cations that coordinate with K+ ions and incorporate Sb3+ in the hybrid framework, thereby strengthening chirality transfer, balancing electric and magnetic transition dipole moments, achieving near-unity photoluminescence quantum yields together with high circularly polarized luminescence dissymmetry factors, and establishing a structure–chiroptical property correlation for high-performance CPL emitter design.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A real bridge pattern in the paper is that molecular-level cation selection and coordination can tune inorganic-hybrid structural dimensionality, which then changes chirality transfer and the balance of electric and magnetic transition dipole moments governing CPL performance."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Incorporating chiral organic cations into metal-halide lattices can impart inherent chirality, and asymmetric hydrogen bonds between chiral cations and halides can exacerbate lattice distortion and deform excited states."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) The deformed excited states associated with asymmetric hydrogen-bond-induced lattice distortion can induce self-trapped excitons that initiate efficient chiral luminescent emission."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) The study reports a structural-dimension regulation strategy in which size- and polarization-tuned chiral organic cations containing oxygen heteroatoms coordinate with K+ to rationally design 0D, 1D, and 3D indium-based hybrids."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) The reported hybrids show bright near-unity PLQY, and the paper attributes overcoming the PLQY-g_lum tradeoff to structural-dimension regulation that enhances chiral induction and balances μ and m."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) In these lead-free indium-based chiral metal-halide hybrids, regulating structural dimensionality through tuned chiral organic cations is associated with near-unity PLQY, enhanced chiral induction, balanced electric and magnetic transition dipole moments, and large g_lum/FOM values."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) Because g_lum is defined through μ, m, and θ, a condition in which |μ| is much larger than |m| represents a mismatch between electric and magnetic transition dipole moments that is relevant to weak CPL dissymmetry."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) The potential applications of CPL-active chiral materials, together with the use of g_lum as a quantitative CPL metric involving electric and magnetic transition dipole moments, motivate attention to CPL material performance."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) Traditional CPL-active materials are commonly described as limited by high cost, poor thermal stability, and low luminescence intensity."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) Reported 0D indium-antimony chlorides with near-unity STE emission and g_lum of about ±1.0×10^-2 illustrate progress in chiral lead-free metal-halide CPL materials, while CPL performance is still described as not being governed by specific rules."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) Chiral lead-free metal halides have achieved significant CPL performance advances, but the literature is characterized as lacking specific rules and often relying on trial-and-error exploration."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) Because CPL performance is described as lacking specific rules, clarifying the structure–chiroptical property relationship is needed to guide efficient circularly polarized light source design."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) A small g_lum value caused by mismatch between electric and magnetic transition dipole moments exemplifies why balancing g_lum and PLQY through μ and m modulation is a design challenge."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Although excellent CPL-active materials have been reported, inorganic nanomaterials can require coating with chiral ligands and can have difficult-to-control properties."];
  "c_ideal" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given the rule if to achieve both polarization purity and energy conversion effectiveness then ideal emitter has large g_lum and high PLQY with FOM = g_lum × PLQY, and currently there is need for polarization purity and energy conversion effectiveness, therefore ideal emitter should have large g_lum and high PLQY."];
  "c_promise" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given the phenomenon lead-free hybrids as hotspot with low cost/high PLQY/diverse structures, and knowledge that CISS endows stable intrinsic chirality with excellent CPL, therefore promising candidates for high-performance emitters."];
  "c_small" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given the rule if |μ| much larger than |m| then mismatch leads to g_lum around 10^{-4} to 10^{-3}, and currently |μ| much larger than |m|, therefore g_lum is small around 10^{-4} to 10^{-3}."];
  "s1" [label="(1,0,0) Chiral materials featuring CPL are garnering significant attention for potential applications in 3D displays, quantum computing, spintronic devices, and information storage."];
  "s10" [label="(10,0,0) Inorganic nanomaterials require coating with chiral ligands and properties challenging to control."];
  "s11" [label="(11,0,0) Lead-free metal halide hybrids hotspot due to low cost, facile synthesis, diverse structures, high PLQY."];
  "s12" [label="(12,0,0) CISS effect endows more stable intrinsic chirality with excellent CPL behavior."];
  "s13" [label="(13,0,0) Directly incorporating chiral organic cations into lattice imparts inherent chirality."];
  "s14" [label="(14,0,0) Asymmetric hydrogen bonds exacerbate lattice distortion leading to deformation of excited state."];
  "s15" [label="(15,0,0) Deformed excited state induces STEs for efficient chiral luminescent emission."];
  "s16" [label="(16,0,0) Several significant achievements realized in chiral lead-free metal halides."];
  "s17" [label="(17,0,0) Quan et al. reported 0D indium-antimony chlorides with near-unity STEs and g_lum ±1.0×10^{-2}."];
  "s2" [label="(2,0,0) g_lum is key metric obtained from equation involving μ, m, θ."];
  "s22" [label="(22,0,0) CPL performance not governed by specific rules, typically trial-and-error."];
  "s23" [label="(23,0,0) Highly needed to clarify structure-chiroptical properties relation and provide guidance."];
  "s24" [label="(24,0,0) Efficient strategy developed to achieve high PLQY and large g_lum by regulating structural dimensions."];
  "s25" [label="(25,0,0) 0D, 1D, 3D In-based hybrids rationally designed by tuning size and polarization of chiral organic cations with oxygen heteroatom coordinating K+."];
  "s26" [label="(26,0,0) Bright emission with near-unity PLQY obtained in the hybrids."];
  "s27" [label="(27,0,0) Contradiction between PLQY and g_lum overcome by regulating structural dimensions enhancing chiral induction and balancing μ and m."];
  "s3" [label="(3,0,0) |μ| much larger than |m| leading to mismatch."];
  "s4" [label="(4,0,0) Ideal CPL emitter should have large g_lum and high PLQY."];
  "s5" [label="(5,0,0) FOM = g_lum × PLQY to quantify performance."];
  "s6" [label="(6,0,0) Design challenges lie in balancing g_lum and PLQY via modulating μ and m."];
  "s7" [label="(7,0,0) Numerous researches led to excellent CPL-active materials including organic small molecules, inorganic nanomaterials, and metal complexes."];
  "s8" [label="(8,0,0) Most barely promising due to high cost, poor thermal stability and luminescence intensity."];
